  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 PM EDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nOnly real issue in the near term is lower dew points this afternoon \nthanks to strong dry air mix down signal, and the potential for some \npatchy valley frost, and/or river valley fog in our eastern valleys \ntonight. Will issue an SPS to cover those two issues in coordination \nwith ILN/JKL. Expecting some upper sky cover toward dawn across the \nwestern CWA, so a bit more confident in these threats east of I-65.\n\nOtherwise, dry and pleasant weather looks to hold for the entire \nweek as expansive surface high pressure controls much of the eastern \nCONUS. Meanwhile, a strong upper ridge axis will stretch from the \nRio Grande up through the Great Lakes region. As we move into the \nweekend, dry and pleasant weather continues as the upper ridge takes \non more of an Omega Block look. \n\nExpecting plenty of sunshine through the week which will allow for \ntemperatures to push up slightly above normal for this time of year. \nHighs should mostly be in the upper 60s and low 70s through the \nweek, however with the stronger ridge by the weekend we could push \nmore into the low and mid 70s. This is a little above the expected \nnormals for this time of year in the mid and upper 60s. Overnight \nlows will mostly be in the low and mid 40s through the week, however \nwith good radiational cooling some of the coolest/sheltered valleys \nmay still find the upper 30s. Some patchy frost may be possible in \nour coolest eastern valleys.\n\nThe other changes made to the forecast involved lowering dew points \neach afternoon and early evening through the week. Deeper mixing \nwill allow for some dry air mix down potential from the hydrolapse, \nso have mirrored dew points closer to the 19/12z Canadian model in \ncoordination with surrounding offices.\n\n===== Worsening Drought Conditions ====\n\nWhile the beautiful fall weather will be nice to have, it does come \nwith a catch. This prolonged dry period could worsen drought \nconditions for our area. Almost our entire forecast area is now \nexperiencing at least abnormally dry (D0) conditions, with south-\ncentral KY even worse with moderate drought (D1). Since Sept 1, \nLouisville (SDF) and Lexington are both running more than 2.5\" below \nnormal rainfall for this time frame. Bowling Green is only behind \n0.58\" since Sept 1, but the bigger picture shows they are 5.25\" \nbelow normal rainfall since the start of 2026. With no rainfall \nexpected in the next seven days, these climatological departures \nfrom normal will only continue to spread.",
